A||||7||mrgone||distributer cap stuck on, cant get it off   :(||||9||
Z||000000||mrgone||05-07-2001||12:07 AM||||the wise-guy who abused my car before me used 2 different types of bolts (one is a screw) to attach the distributer cap. the bolt comes off, but the screw is so rusted it has no way of me getting it off...<BR>please answer all my q's as this could become a big inconvience. ie 'reliable transportation required for new job'<P>this tells me one thing<BR>if the screw is rusty that bad, the cap is old and needs replacing.<P>how long do i have until it fails and im stranded<P>whats the procedure of.... getting this thing off<P>would it be costly for a shop to do it?<BR>or would the safest bet be to drive my butt down to GM<P>and would the dealership give me a loaner car even if im only 18 years old, if the car is in the shop during my work week<P>||||reg||9
Z||000001||skitime||05-07-2001||08:23 AM||||You have 63 days 5 hours and 3 minutes until distributor cap failure.  A hammer ought to get the cap off.  The shops probably already have a hammer to get it off so it will cost less for them to do it.  I am not sure why GM would want your cigarette butt however it would be safe to drive it there if it is too far to walk.  I would doubt the dealership would loan an 18 year old anything except a pen to write a check for a huge repair bill and cigarette butt removal.  <BR>Sorry just having fun, I would carefully mark the base of the distributor so it can be reinstalled accurately. I would then remove the distributor and break the cap around the rusted screw and remove it with a vise grip. Clean up the threads and install new cap-rotor then reinstall distributor.  Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||05-07-2001||09:59 AM||||If the previous owner didnt use the screw that came with the distributor cap, I would suspect maybe the threads in the block were stripped out and he put in and oversized screw. If so its not a big deal, you might need to drill out a slightly bigger hole in new distributor caps from now on, and you might want to tap the hole properly so screws wont get stuck in there anymore.<P>  The last poster is correct. If you cant get the screw out by normal means crush the cap with vise grips, then put the vice grips on the part of the screw that is left and carefully turn it out. <P>  Same goes for the rotor. I absolutely could not pull the rotor off on the car I just bought, so I took a big pair of vise grips and crushed the plastic on the distributor shaft part of it.<P>   If you find yourself reaching for the BFH (hammer) grab a chisle too, and try to be as carefull as possible if you have to bust up the cap to get it off. There are coils and moving parts at the base of the distributor that you dont want to ruin.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrgone||05-07-2001||12:45 PM||||if i get it back today... i take a pic of it.<P>i hope i dont ruin it||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||05-07-2001||02:14 PM||||BTW taking your car to the GM dealer for service is the most expensive way to get 'proper' repairs done. <P>taking you car to a franchise repair shop is the most expensive way to get stuck with a huge bill for repairs you didnt acutally need.<P>If you are gonna own a Fiero I strongly suggest you try to tackle as much repair work as you think you can handle, and find a good mechanic you can trust for the stuff you cant.<P>I had to take my saturn to the dealer to get the inside door handle replaced (it crushed when I leaned on it to get change out of my pocket at a toll booth - turns out its a safety thing, your not suppose to lean on them). But they will replace it this once under the 3 year warrenty.<P>While I was there I asked how much it would cost for them to replace the CAT on my 94 saturn SL2 (which is ratteling inside).<P>  I know I can get the CAT and do it myself for about $100.<P>  they would do it for $725.  Yhow!||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||05-07-2001||04:32 PM||||I have had mixed results with dealerships.  Every so often, I delude myself into thinking that taking a car in for repairs will save time and/or money.  Just recently, I took my car into a dealership for work, because I don't have many tools with me.  Not to name names, but their initials are Clyde-Revolting.  Or something like that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Anyway, enough personal whining.  For simple repairs like a seized distributor cap screw, I suspect you'll be much better off fixing it yourself.  It's scarier, but you'll very likely learn something (at the very least, who you can ask for a ride to work!), get a more satisfactory repair, and get it done more quickly and <B>much</B> cheaper.  Just Stucents' worth.      ||||reg||4
Z||000006||Nashco||05-07-2001||11:28 PM||||NO THEY WILL NOT GIVE YOU A LOANER CAR.  Just talking from experience, because the totally shltty dealership down here wouldn't give me one during the two week period they needed it.  Oh, and it's no secret, RON NEWTON AND HIS ASSOCIATES SUCK!  Ok, now that I got that out of the system, don't ever take your car to Ron Newton anything.  Oh, and Clyde Revord?  What were you thinking?  Hehe, used to live in Snohomish, so I know.  My dad is an awesome Fiero mechanic if you're interested StuGood.  He still lives in Snohomish, and I might be able to talk him into it if you need anything done.  Seriously, he's the best if you need something done.  Anyway..I just wish I would have had somebody to help me out personally.<P>Oh, and yes, a hammer is the same thing a dealership will use.  Distributor caps are old school, there's plenty of help available if you need it.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||05-08-2001||09:00 AM||||If you are going to <BR>take the distributor cap off<BR>with a hammer<BR>be sure to follow<BR>the ritualistic proceedure.<P>Its best if you can get a real <BR>jack in the box<BR>and a helper to turn the crank<BR>while you stand ready with the hammer<P>(it you cant get one<BR>get a recording of one<BR>and play it on the car stereo<BR>or as a last resort<BR>just sing it yourself<BR>not the words<BR>only the du dunt du dunt du du due du dunt<BR>part)<P>then just at the right instant<BR>POP! goes the distributor cap<P>Works every time!<P>(ya gotta have a sense of humor if you own a fiero)||||reg||1
